ouadi Maakoul
with Gemini, DeepSeek, and ChatGPT

---

Abstract

This thesis develops a deductive mathematical framework for the hypothesis that aging is driven by a positive feedback loop: senescent cells secrete a diffusible signal that induces senescence in bystander cells, propagating the phenotype systemically. No empirical data or numerical simulations are used. A minimal system of autonomous ordinary differential equations is postulated and subjected to complete analytical treatment: steady‑state identification, linear stability via cooperative systems theory and the Routh–Hurwitz criterion, bifurcation characterisation, and deduction of intervention consequences from phase‑space geometry. A dimensionless reduction isolates a basic feedback number R_0. In the affine‑linear core, a stable healthy equilibrium exists exactly when R_0<1 and escapes to infinity at R_0=1. To enforce the physical bound 0\le s\le 1 and immune saturation we introduce a pool‑limitation factor (1-s) and a clearance term comprising a basal linear rate plus a saturable Michaelis–Menten component. The resulting nonlinear system possesses steady states governed by a cubic polynomial. Sufficient conditions for the existence of exactly three distinct positive equilibria are established via discriminant analysis and sign alternation at critical points, yielding bistability and hysteresis. The separatrix dividing healthy and pathological basins is identified as the stable manifold of the intermediate saddle, giving an exact geometric definition of the “point of no return.” The theory predicts a therapeutic window for senolytics, critical slowing down as a dynamical biomarker, and synergy between senolytics and signal neutralisation—all deduced rigorously from the mathematical structure. The work provides an internally self‑consistent minimal framework that renders the senescence‑propagation hypothesis precise, transparent, and falsifiable.

---

1. Axiomatic Foundations and Model Construction

1.1 Conceptual Premise and Scope

We formalise the hypothesis that organismal aging is substantially driven by a positive feedback loop: senescent cells secrete a diffusible senescence‑associated factor that induces senescence in non‑senescent cells, thereby propagating the senescent phenotype systemically. This work is entirely theoretical. We propose a minimal set of ordinary differential equations encoding the hypothesised architecture and analyse them analytically—identifying steady states, determining their stability, characterising bifurcations, and deducing intervention consequences directly from the analytic properties of the system.

1.2 State Variables

Four non‑negative, time‑dependent, aggregated variables are defined:

· D(t) — cumulative molecular damage (dimensionless);
· S(t) — senescent cell burden, the fraction of cells in a senescent state (0\le S\le 1);
· H(t) — concentration of a representative circulating senescence‑associated factor (arbitrary units, subsequently rescaled);
· I(t) — systemic inflammation index (dimensionless).

We work in the mean‑field limit, neglecting spatial heterogeneity and stochastic fluctuations.

1.3 Dynamical Laws

The governing autonomous system is

\begin{aligned}
\frac{dD}{dt} &= \omega - \tau D, \\
\frac{dS}{dt} &= \alpha D + \beta H + \gamma I - \delta S, \\
\frac{dH}{dt} &= \kappa S - \lambda H, \\
\frac{dI}{dt} &= \rho S + \sigma H - \mu I,
\end{aligned}

where all parameters are strictly positive. Biologically, \omega is the damage accumulation rate, \tau the repair rate, \alpha,\beta,\gamma the efficiencies of senescence induction by damage, signal, and inflammation, \delta the basal senescent cell clearance rate, \kappa the per‑capita secretion rate of the signal, \lambda its clearance rate, \rho the rate of inflammation induction by senescent cells, \sigma the rate by the signal, and \mu the inflammation resolution rate. The equations are affine‑linear: coupling terms are linear and the only inhomogeneity is \omega. This structure isolates the feedback topology and makes the Jacobian constant.

1.4 Dimensional Reduction

Adopt the reference time T = 1/\delta and define dimensionless variables

d = \frac{\tau}{\omega}D,\quad
s = \frac{\delta}{\alpha\omega/\tau}S,\quad
h = \frac{\lambda}{\kappa}\frac{\delta}{\alpha\omega/\tau}H,\quad
i = \frac{\mu}{\rho}\frac{\delta}{\alpha\omega/\tau}I,\quad
\tilde{t} = \delta t.

Then

\begin{aligned}
\frac{dd}{d\tilde{t}} &= \epsilon(1-d), \\
\frac{ds}{d\tilde{t}} &= d + b h + c i - s, \\
\frac{dh}{d\tilde{t}} &= \eta(s-h), \\
\frac{di}{d\tilde{t}} &= \zeta(s+\theta h - i),
\end{aligned}

with dimensionless parameters

\epsilon=\frac{\tau}{\delta},\; b=\frac{\beta\kappa}{\lambda\delta},\; c=\frac{\gamma\rho}{\mu\delta},\; \eta=\frac{\lambda}{\delta},\; \zeta=\frac{\mu}{\delta},\; \theta=\frac{\sigma\kappa}{\rho\lambda}.

For \epsilon\ll1 (slow damage repair), d\to1 rapidly on the timescale of the senescence–signal–inflammation subsystem; setting d\equiv1 reduces the core to the three‑dimensional affine system

\begin{aligned}
\frac{ds}{d\tilde{t}} &= 1 + b h + c i - s, \\
\frac{dh}{d\tilde{t}} &= \eta(s-h), \\
\frac{di}{d\tilde{t}} &= \zeta(s+\theta h - i).
\end{aligned}

The whole qualitative dynamics are thus controlled by the five dimensionless groups b,c,\eta,\zeta,\theta.

