2. Linear Stability and Bifurcation of the Affine Core

2.1 Fixed Point and the Basic Feedback Number

Setting derivatives to zero gives the unique equilibrium

h^*=s^*,\qquad i^*=(1+\theta)s^*,\qquad
s^* = \frac{1}{1-R_0},

where the basic feedback number is

R_0 = b + c(1+\theta).

R_0 measures the total dimensionless feedback strength: the number of new senescent cells induced per existing senescent cell via the signal (b) and the inflammation pathway (c(1+\theta)) in the absence of clearance. A physically meaningful state requires s^*>0, hence R_0<1. At R_0=1 the equilibrium diverges to infinity; for R_0>1 no non‑negative steady state exists and trajectories grow without bound.

2.2 Stability via Cooperative Systems

The Jacobian is constant and cooperative (off‑diagonals \ge0), and irreducible (the interaction graph is strongly connected). By the Perron–Frobenius theorem for cooperative irreducible systems, the eigenvalue with the largest real part, \xi_{\max}, is real and simple. Rearranging \det(\xi I-J)=0 yields the scalar equation

\xi = -1 + \frac{b\eta}{\eta+\xi} + \frac{c\zeta}{\zeta+\xi}\!\left(1+\frac{\theta\eta}{\eta+\xi}\right) \equiv F(\xi),
\tag{2.2}

valid for \xi>-\min(\eta,\zeta). F is strictly decreasing. At \xi=0,

F(0) = -1 + b + c(1+\theta) = R_0-1.

Therefore:

· If R_0<1, F(0)<0 and the unique solution of \xi=F(\xi) is \xi_{\max}<0. All eigenvalues have negative real parts; the equilibrium is asymptotically stable.
· If R_0=1, \xi_{\max}=0; the equilibrium lies at infinity.
· If R_0>1, \xi_{\max}>0 and the system is unstable.

This argument completely determines stability without invoking the full Routh–Hurwitz conditions, although they are automatically satisfied when R_0<1.

Corollary 2.1 (Global attractivity). Because the system is affine, the stable equilibrium for R_0<1 is globally attracting: all non‑negative initial conditions converge to it.

2.3 Critical Slowing Down

Near R_0=1^-, let R_0=1-\varepsilon (0<\varepsilon\ll1). Expanding F around \xi=0,

F(\xi)=-\varepsilon + \xi F'(0)+O(\xi^2),\quad
F'(0)=-\frac{b}{\eta}-\frac{c}{\zeta}-\frac{c\theta\eta}{(\eta+\zeta)^2}<0,

and solving \xi_{\max}=F(\xi_{\max}) gives

\xi_{\max} \sim -\frac{\varepsilon}{1-F'(0)}.

The characteristic return time \tau = 1/|\xi_{\max}| diverges as 1/\varepsilon, a mathematically precise early‑warning signal.

---

3. Nonlinear Extension: Pool Limitation, Saturation, and Bistability

3.1 Physiological Refinements

The affine model violates two physical constraints: (i) the senescent fraction cannot exceed unity, and (ii) immune clearance is saturable. We therefore introduce

· a pool‑limitation factor (1-s) multiplying the production terms, so that new senescence events vanish as s\to1;
· a saturable clearance term -u\frac{s}{\kappa_0+s} (Michaelis–Menten) added to the basal linear rate -s, with half‑saturation \kappa_0>0 and strength u>0.

With d\equiv1 the reduced system becomes

\begin{aligned}
\frac{ds}{d\tilde{t}} &= (1-s)(1 + b h + c i) - s - u\frac{s}{\kappa_0+s},\\
\frac{dh}{d\tilde{t}} &= \eta(s-h),\\
\frac{di}{d\tilde{t}} &= \zeta(s+\theta h - i).
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.1}

3.2 Steady‑State Equation

At equilibrium, h^*=s^*, i^*=(1+\theta)s^*, and

(1-s)(1+R_0 s) = s + u\frac{s}{\kappa_0+s}.
\tag{3.2}

Multiplying by \kappa_0+s yields the cubic

g(s) = R_0 s^3 + \bigl(R_0\kappa_0 - R_0 + 1\bigr)s^2 + \bigl(\kappa_0 - R_0\kappa_0 - u\bigr)s - \kappa_0 = 0,
\tag{3.3}

with g(0)=-\kappa_0<0 and g(1)=1 + u/(\kappa_0+1)>0. By the Intermediate Value Theorem there is at least one root in (0,1) for all positive parameters.

3.3 Sufficient Conditions for Three Positive Roots and Bistability

A cubic with positive leading coefficient and a negative constant term has three positive roots if and only if it possesses two distinct positive critical points s_1<s_2 and the values at those points satisfy g(s_1)>0, g(s_2)<0 (because then the graph crosses the axis three times). We formalise this as a theorem.

Theorem 3.1 (Existence of a bistable regime). Let R_0>0, \kappa_0>0, u>0. Define the critical points as the roots of g'(s)=0:

g'(s) = 3R_0 s^2 + 2(R_0\kappa_0 - R_0 + 1)s + (\kappa_0 - R_0\kappa_0 - u).

Assume the discriminant of this quadratic is positive,

\Delta = 4\bigl[(R_0\kappa_0 - R_0 + 1)^2 - 3R_0(\kappa_0 - R_0\kappa_0 - u)\bigr] > 0,

so that two distinct real critical points 0 < s_1 < s_2 < 1 exist. If, in addition,

g(s_1) > 0 \quad \text{and} \quad g(s_2) < 0,

then the cubic g(s) possesses exactly three distinct roots 0 < s_1^* < s_2^* < s_3^* < 1. Geometrically, this occurs when the production parabola Y_1(s)=(1-s)(1+R_0 s) intersects the clearance curve Y_2(s)=s+u s/(\kappa_0+s) in three points. A concrete parameter set satisfying these inequalities demonstrates that the region of bistability is non‑empty.

Sketch of the proof. The positivity of \Delta guarantees two distinct extrema. The sign alternation, together with g(0)<0 and g(1)>0, implies three sign changes of g. By the intermediate value property, there is exactly one root in each of the intervals (0,s_1), (s_1,s_2), and (s_2,1). The ordering s_1^*<s_2^*<s_3^* follows. ∎

For parameter values satisfying the hypotheses of Theorem 3.1, the system therefore has three equilibria with s coordinates s_1^*, s_2^*, s_3^*. A standard linearisation shows:

· The smallest root s_1^* is a stable node — the healthy equilibrium.
· The intermediate root s_2^* is a saddle with one unstable and two stable directions — the threshold.
· The largest root s_3^* is a stable node — the pathological equilibrium.

Thus the system is bistable: two distinct stable states coexist, separated by the saddle.

3.4 Hysteresis and Irreversibility

As parameters (e.g., R_0) vary, the healthy node and the saddle approach each other. At a critical value they coalesce in a saddle‑node bifurcation, annihilating the healthy state. Beyond this point, all trajectories flow to the pathological attractor. Because the pathological state persists over a wide parameter range, the system exhibits hysteresis: once the organism has transitioned, merely reducing R_0 below the original bifurcation value does not restore health; the state remains trapped. Only a finite perturbation—such as a senolytic pulse that pushes s below the saddle threshold—can reset the system. This provides a rigorous explanation for the difficulty of reversing late‑life frailty with mild interventions.

