4. Intervention Logic from Phase‑Space Geometry

4.1 Senolytic Pulse

A fast‑acting senolytic is idealised as an instantaneous map

s \mapsto (1-f)s,\quad h\mapsto h,\quad i\mapsto i,
\qquad 0\le f\le 1,

leaving the extracellular signal and inflammation unchanged.

4.2 The Point of No Return

In the bistable regime, the saddle point \mathbf{P}_{\text{saddle}}=(s_2^*,s_2^*,(1+\theta)s_2^*)^\top possesses a two‑dimensional stable manifold W^s(\mathbf{P}_{\text{saddle}}) that forms the global separatrix dividing the phase space into basins of attraction of the healthy and pathological nodes. Because the system is cooperative and irreducible, no other invariant sets exist.

Let \mathbf{x}_0=(s_0,h_0,i_0)^\top be the pre‑intervention state and \mathbf{x}_{\text{post}}=((1-f)s_0,h_0,i_0)^\top. Durable therapeutic success is equivalent to \mathbf{x}_{\text{post}}\in\mathcal{B}(\mathbf{P}_{\text{healthy}}). The point of no return is the moment when h_0 and i_0 have accumulated so much that even total senescent cell clearance (f=1, giving (0,h_0,i_0)^\top) leaves the state on the pathological side of the separatrix; the feedback then rapidly restores the senescent burden. This yields a precise geometric therapeutic window: early intervention, when the signalling components are low, can achieve lasting reset; late intervention cannot.

4.3 Synergy with Signal Neutralisation

Sustained signal neutralisation (e.g., antibodies against the SASP factor) permanently reduces b or c, lowering R_0 to R_0'<R_0. This has two effects:

· It can restore a healthy equilibrium if the original parameter point lay beyond the saddle‑node bifurcation.
· It shifts the saddle s_2^* rightwards, expanding the healthy basin \mathcal{B}(\mathbf{P}_{\text{healthy}}).

Consequently, a combined intervention—senolytic pulse plus signal neutralisation—is synergistic: the expanded basin raises the chance that the post‑pulse state falls into the healthy region, even when the pulse alone would fail. This deduction is purely geometric.

4.4 Critical Slowing Down as a Dynamical Biomarker

Near the saddle‑node bifurcation, the dominant eigenvalue of the healthy node tends to zero. The return time \tau_{\text{return}}=1/|\xi_{\max}| diverges. Tracking the recovery rate of systemic markers (e.g., circulating signal or inflammatory indices) after minor transient perturbations thus provides a non‑invasive, early‑warning biomarker of imminent collapse, independent of detailed parameter knowledge.

---

5. Discussion

We have constructed a mathematically rigorous, minimal framework for the hypothesis that senescence propagation via a diffusible signal contributes to systemic aging. The principal results are:

· The identification of a basic feedback number R_0 = b + c(1+\theta) and the proof that the affine core possesses a globally stable healthy equilibrium exactly when R_0<1.
· A physiologically bounded nonlinear extension that respects s\in[0,1] and includes both basal and saturable immune clearance, guaranteeing the existence of steady states for all parameters.
· Sufficient analytical conditions for the emergence of exactly three positive steady states, yielding bistability, hysteresis, and an unstable saddle threshold.
· The geometric definition of the “point of no return” as the stable manifold of the saddle, and the deduction of therapeutic windows, synergy, and critical slowing down as a biomarker.

The theory employs no numerical data; it is a formal logical structure that makes the underlying assumptions explicit and derives their inevitable consequences. If the feedback loop modelled here captures a core truth of senescent cell biology, then the predicted phenomena—saddle‑node bifurcations, basin boundaries, hysteresis, and critical slowing down—must be observable. The framework thus provides sharp, falsifiable targets for experimental inquiry. Should those phenomena not appear, the model’s premises must be revised. In either case, the architecture organises thought and guides investigation.

The work constitutes an internally self‑consistent, analytically closed minimal core. Future extensions to spatial diffusion, stochastic effects, and multiple SASP factors remain open, but the present contribution stands as a rigorous logical skeleton for the systemic dynamics of aging.

