Appendix A: Routh–Hurwitz Stability Proof for the Affine Core

The Jacobian of the reduced affine system (1.9)–(1.11) is

J = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 & b & c \\
\eta & -\eta & 0 \\
\zeta & \zeta\theta & -\zeta
\end{pmatrix}.

The characteristic polynomial is P(\xi)=\xi^3 + a_1 \xi^2 + a_2 \xi + a_3 with

\begin{aligned}
a_1 &= 1+\eta+\zeta,\\[2pt]
a_2 &= \eta+\zeta+\eta\zeta - b\eta - c\zeta,\\[2pt]
a_3 &= \eta\zeta(1-b-c-c\theta) = \eta\zeta(1-R_0).
\end{aligned}

All parameters are positive, so a_1>0. By the Routh–Hurwitz criterion for a real cubic, all eigenvalues have strictly negative real parts if and only if

a_1>0,\qquad a_3>0,\qquad a_1 a_2 > a_3.

The condition a_3>0 is equivalent to R_0<1. It remains to verify a_1 a_2 > a_3 whenever R_0<1. We prove this by direct algebraic manipulation.

Let R_0 = 1-\delta with \delta\in(0,1]. Then a_3 = \eta\zeta\delta. The inequality a_1 a_2 > a_3 becomes

(1+\eta+\zeta)(\eta+\zeta+\eta\zeta - b\eta - c\zeta) > \eta\zeta(1-R_0) = \eta\zeta\delta.

Using the definition b + c(1+\theta) = 1-\delta, we can bound b\eta + c\zeta. Because b\le 1-\delta and c\le 1-\delta (as \theta\ge0), we have

b\eta + c\zeta \le (1-\delta)(\eta+\zeta).

Hence

\begin{aligned}
a_1 a_2 &\ge (1+\eta+\zeta)\bigl[\eta+\zeta+\eta\zeta - (1-\delta)(\eta+\zeta)\bigr] \\
&= (1+\eta+\zeta)\bigl[\eta\zeta + \delta(\eta+\zeta)\bigr] \\
&= (1+\eta+\zeta)\eta\zeta + \delta(1+\eta+\zeta)(\eta+\zeta).
\end{aligned}

Clearly (1+\eta+\zeta)\eta\zeta > \eta\zeta\delta for \delta<1 because 1+\eta+\zeta > 1 \ge \delta. The additional positive term \delta(1+\eta+\zeta)(\eta+\zeta) only strengthens the inequality. Thus a_1 a_2 > a_3 for all R_0<1, completing the proof. The equilibrium is asymptotically stable exactly when R_0<1. At R_0=1, a_3=0 and the polynomial factors as \xi(\xi^2+a_1\xi+a_2)=0; the quadratic has positive coefficients, so the non‑zero roots have negative real parts, confirming a simple zero eigenvalue.

