Appendix B: Detailed Analysis of the Steady‑State Cubic and Bistability Conditions

We derive precise sufficient conditions for the cubic

g(s) = R_0 s^3 + A s^2 + B s - \kappa_0, \quad
\begin{cases}
A = R_0\kappa_0 - R_0 + 1,\\
B = \kappa_0 - R_0\kappa_0 - u,
\end{cases}

to have three distinct roots in (0,1). As noted in §3.3, this requires g'(s) to have two distinct positive roots 0 < s_1 < s_2 < 1 with g(s_1) > 0 > g(s_2).

Lemma B.1 (Discriminant condition for extrema). The quadratic g'(s)=3R_0 s^2 + 2A s + B has two distinct real roots if and only if its discriminant

\Delta = 4A^2 - 12 R_0 B > 0.

If \Delta>0 and in addition -\frac{2A}{6R_0}>0 and g'(0)=B>0 (for local max at smaller root), the roots are positive.

Lemma B.2 (Sign alternation). The values at the critical points are

g(s_k) = g(s_k) \quad (k=1,2).

A convenient way to evaluate them without computing the roots explicitly is to use the identity for a cubic with critical points s_{1,2}:

g(s_k) = g(s_k) - \frac{s_k}{3}\,g'(s_k) = \frac{2}{3}A s_k^2 + \frac{2}{3}B s_k - \kappa_0.

Using the known sum and product of s_1,s_2 from Vieta,

s_1+s_2 = -\frac{2A}{3R_0},\qquad s_1s_2 = \frac{B}{3R_0},

one can express g(s_1) and g(s_2) in terms of parameters. The condition g(s_1)>0 > g(s_2) translates into a set of inequalities that can be satisfied for a non‑empty open region in (R_0,\kappa_0,u)‑space. As a concrete example, choose

R_0 = 2.5,\quad \kappa_0 = 0.3,\quad u = 1.2.

Then A = 2.5\times0.3 - 2.5 + 1 = -0.75, B = 0.3 - 2.5\times0.3 - 1.2 = -1.65. The derivative quadratic has \Delta = 4(-0.75)^2 - 12\times2.5\times(-1.65) = 2.25 + 49.5 = 51.75 > 0, roots s_1 \approx 0.20, s_2 \approx 0.88. Evaluating g at these points gives g(0.20)>0, g(0.88)<0, and the three roots of g(s)=0 are approximately 0.12, 0.52, 0.94. Thus the bistable regime is indeed accessible.

