Appendix C: Stability of the Nonlinear Equilibria

Linearising system (3.1) about an equilibrium (s^*, s^*, (1+\theta)s^*) yields the Jacobian

J(s^*) = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 - b h^* - c i^* - 1 - \frac{u\kappa_0}{(\kappa_0+s^*)^2} & (1-s^*)b & (1-s^*)c \\
\eta & -\eta & 0 \\
\zeta & \zeta\theta & -\zeta
\end{pmatrix}.

Using the steady‑state relation (1-s^*)(1+R_0 s^*) = s^* + \frac{u s^*}{\kappa_0+s^*}, the (1,1) entry simplifies, but it remains strictly negative. The off‑diagonals are all non‑negative. Hence J(s^*) is again cooperative and irreducible. By the same Perron–Frobenius argument, its dominant eigenvalue \xi_{\max}(s^*) is real and determines stability. One can show that \xi_{\max}(s^*) < 0 for the smallest and largest roots, and \xi_{\max}(s^*) > 0 for the intermediate root, establishing the stable–saddle–stable pattern. The proof follows from the index theory of cooperative systems: the number of positive eigenvalues (inertia) is constant within each connected component of equilibria separated by folds; at the folds a simple eigenvalue crosses zero, changing stability. Because the cubic has three branches, the middle branch must have exactly one positive eigenvalue.

