Appendix D: Cooperative Systems and Global Separatrix

The reduced three‑dimensional system, both affine and nonlinear, is cooperative: the off‑diagonal entries of the Jacobian are non‑negative everywhere in the non‑negative orthant. Moreover, the interaction graph is strongly connected, so the Jacobian is irreducible. For such systems, the flow is monotone with respect to the standard cone \mathbb{R}^3_+. This implies that there are no attracting periodic orbits, and that the only limit sets are equilibria. Consequently, the stable manifold of the saddle point is a codimension‑one surface that separates the basins of attraction of the two stable nodes—exactly the geometric separatrix invoked in §4.2. These properties are standard in the theory of cooperative and irreducible dynamical systems; we refer the reader to the monograph by Smith (1995, Monotone Dynamical Systems) for proofs and further details.


